y servants, a grey throne where the Frost Giant sat, holding the stolen starlight of the Briar Vale in a jar. 'Mine,' the Frost Giant hissed. 'All mine.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'Starlight Restored', 'text': "the Frost Giant's grumble was kept in a jar on the mantel, as a reminder. On quiet nights it mutters. Ash finds it comforting: proof that dark can be made small, and kept.\n\nbe brave, be kind — Ash wrote it in the family book, under the date, in the careful letters of someone who means it forever.\n\nThey carried the starlight home to the Briar Vale in a feather of fire, which had grown to hold it all. The brook ran forward again. The mushrooms glowed gold. The stars came back, one by one, like friends returning."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise Kept', 'text': "a loyal dog is older now, greyer, no less brave. They still walk together, still argue about the ballad, still keep the grumble-jar dusted. Friendship, like starlight, doesn't fade if you tend it.\n\nAsh grew, as heroes do, but never grew out of the Briar Vale. Every spring they check the old oak. Every night a feather of fire glows. Every tale a loyal dog tells starts, 'When we saved the stars...'\n\nthe Briar Vale thrives: the enchanted forest enchants, the glade whispers kindly, the meadow moonlights. All because one child picked up a feather of fire and one friend said 'together.'"}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Bedtime', 'text': "And now it's bedtime in the Briar Vale, and a feather of fire glows soft, and a loyal dog is telling the story one more time, to one more sleepy listener. 'The end,' a loyal dog says.\n\n'Again,' says the listener. It is always 'again.' The best stories are.\n\nSleep tight, the Briar Vale. The dark is just the blanket. The light is a feather of fire, and a feather of fire is here, and Ash is here, and a loyal dog is here."}]}

